  "live_records": {
    "q-332619": {
      "dom_id": "step-q332619",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]x(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,x(t)={e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem and its solution given above. What is the tangent vector to the solution curve at the point corresponding to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3?$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & ={e}^{-2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+3[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡3) & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2(\\frac{1}{3})+0(6) \\\\ 0(\\frac{1}{3})+1(6)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
